<h1>Susana Mendoza</h1>
<p>Candidate for Mayor, Chicago, on the ballot February 23, 2027</p>
<p><a href="https://susanamendoza.com" rel="nofollow noopener">Campaign website</a></p>
<p><a href="https://susanamendoza.com/priorities/" rel="nofollow noopener">Susana Mendoza&#39;s campaign lists sixteen priorities under three headings: the city&#39;s finances, public safety, and confidence in the city. Tap here to read them on her site, then come back to comment.</a></p>
<h2>AI summary</h2>
<h3>The platform</h3>
<p>Finances: Grow the tax base instead of raising rates, cut waste through a program called TRIM, prepay pensions, and stop using neighborhood TIF money to plug budget holes.</p>
<p>Police: Fill police vacancies, bring back ShotSpotter, put officers on trains, and change state law so people accused of murder or sexual assault cannot be released on electronic monitoring.</p>
<p>Building: A public dashboard comparing Chicago&#39;s permit times with other cities, help for small businesses, and more housing near transit.</p>
<p>Record: Mendoza points to her years as Illinois Comptroller, including ten credit rating upgrades.</p>
<h3>Next to the other candidates</h3>
<p>Only Mendoza: Changing the state&#39;s SAFE-T Act. On ShotSpotter, Mendoza would bring it back; Carlisle opposes that product and wants city-run gunshot detection.</p>
<p>Transit: Mendoza and Cardenas would put police on the CTA, and Kelly wants it safe at every hour. Carlisle and Su would run trains and buses more often.</p>
<p>Shared: Mendoza and Quigley would both stop spending TIF money on the budget. Quigley would send it to affordable housing.</p>
<p>Not covered: Schools, which Holberg, Kelly, Wilson, Brewer, Carlisle, and Su address.</p>
